# Line endings for files a Linux container executes or parses.
#
# core.autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows default) checks text files out with
# CRLF. For anything that only ever runs on the host that is harmless; for a
# file COPYed into an image it is not. Measured 2026-08-28 by converting
# Frontend/docker-entrypoint.d/10-require-tls-cert.sh to CRLF and building:
#
#   /docker-entrypoint.sh: line 31:
#       /docker-entrypoint.d/10-require-tls-cert.sh: not found
#   exit 127
#
# The file is right there. The kernel reads the shebang as `/bin/sh\r`, fails
# to find *that* interpreter, and reports the script as missing -- a message
# that sends you looking for a broken COPY rather than a stray carriage
# return. It reproduces only after a commit and a fresh Windows clone, which
# is precisely the path the rest of this change exists to make work.
#
# Scoped deliberately: no `* text=auto`, which would renormalize the entire
# repo in one commit and bury real changes in whitespace churn.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Same reasoning. CRLF in a Dockerfile breaks backslash line-continuations
# (the continuation swallows the \r and the next line is parsed as a new
# instruction), and nginx.conf is read inside the container too.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
